<p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
    </p>

    ## SECTION 1 — HEADER BANNER
    <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
        <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
        <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
    </div>

    ## SECTION 2 — LESSON IDENTIFICATION TABLE
    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Changing Forests</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Explored the Ecological History Of...</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Concept: Interpret non-verbal cues in communication. Communication: Facilitate peer discussions on various topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can interpret classmates' non-verbal cues, like nodding or frowning, in discussions about environmental issues and adjust responses accordingly (e.g., uses a hand gesture to invite contribution and says, "I noticed you seemed interested; would you like to share your thoughts on recycling?").
                Can facilitate discussions on ecological topics by prompting classmates to contribute ideas about conservation, using visuals like a deforestation map to spark conversation (e.g., "How can we reduce pollution in our area?").
                Can interpret reactions, such as looking puzzled, during discussions on deforestation, using simpler language and drawings to explain (e.g., points to a simple diagram of a forest and says, "Deforestation means cutting down trees, which harms animals' habitats.")</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    ## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
    <h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Display a series of images showing local forests: one healthy and thriving, one showing signs of deforestation or pollution, and one depicting community efforts in conservation (e.g., planting trees). Ask students to observe the images and, without speaking, show with a facial expression or simple gesture how each image makes them feel (e.g., a smile for health, a frown for damage, a thumbs-up for action). This activates prior knowledge on "Our Changing Forests" and introduces non-verbal communication. Prompt students to consider the concept: "I Explored the Ecological History Of..." through these images.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to interpret non-verbal cues and mediate. For instance, the teacher shows an image of a deforested area and makes a confused face. Then, the teacher models saying, "I see some of you look puzzled by this image of logging. It means cutting down trees, which has significantly affected biodiversity. Let me try to explain it more simply: imagine many different species losing their native homes." The teacher then points to a simple drawing of a tree being cut down. Teacher also models polite language: "Protecting our forests benefits everyone."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If someone frowns during a discussion, what might that non-verbal cue mean?" (Expected answer: "They might be confused or disagree.")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What could you do to help a classmate understand a difficult term like 'ecological impact' if they look puzzled?" (Expected answer: "Rephrase it, use a simpler word, or draw a picture.")</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[Date]<br>[Time]</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Changing Forests".</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short blog post excerpt (narrative, informative register) titled "Panama's Forests: A Decade of Change." The text describes how deforestation has affected local biodiversity and how communities are trying to improve forest health. It includes instances where complex environmental terms are used, and then briefly simplified for a general audience. Example sentences: "Over the past decade, our local forests have experienced a significant decline in native species due to increased logging. If we preserve our forests, we will help reduce this dramatic impact. Urban ecosystems have less biodiversity compared to rural habitats."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Facilitate peer discussions on various topics. Interpret non-verbal cues in communication.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the excerpt. Then, in pairs, they identify three complex terms related to "Our Changing Forests" (e.g., 'biodiversity,' 'sustainable,' 'ecological impact') and discuss how a reader might show confusion non-verbally.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students then brainstorm (and practice sharing orally) different ways to simplify these terms, using the provided vocabulary and grammar (present perfect, conditionals, comparatives). E.g., "Deforestation means cutting down trees, which harms animals' habitats."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[Date]<br>[Time]</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Interpreting non-verbal cues and facilitating discussions on environmental issues. Using Present perfect, First and second conditional, and Comparatives. Vocabulary: deforestation, biodiversity, pollution, ecosystems, conservation, forest health, logging, species, impact, reduce, plant, improve, protect, healthier, significant, ecological, native.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: Non-Verbal Cue Match-Up (Controlled Practice).</b> Teacher displays a list of non-verbal cues (e.g., "looking puzzled," "nodding slowly," "shrugging shoulders," "eyes widening"). Students receive cards with appropriate mediating responses (e.g., "I noticed you seemed interested; would you like to share your thoughts?", "Would you like me to explain that differently?", "Perhaps we can look at a visual aid?"). Students work individually to match each cue with the most suitable mediating response. Emphasis on the long vowel sound /aɪ/ in "life," "type."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: Simplifying Environmental Concepts (Semi-controlled).</b> In pairs, students receive a set of complex sentences extracted from the 21st Century Skills Project outline (e.g., "Deforestation has significantly affected biodiversity in our area," "If people planted more trees, forests would recover faster," "Urban ecosystems have less biodiversity compared to rural habitats"). One student reads a sentence, and the other student, acting as a confused peer, displays a puzzled non-verbal cue. The first student must then rephrase the sentence using simpler language or suggest drawing a quick diagram, incorporating the target grammar (present perfect, conditionals, comparatives) and vocabulary (pollution, ecosystems, conservation). Students practice pronunciation of -ate ending as /ɪt/ in words like "climate," "accurate."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: Facilitating a Mini-Discussion (Pair Interaction/Peer Check).</b> Students form new pairs. Each pair is given a topic related to "Our Changing Forests" (e.g., "How can we reduce plastic waste in our rivers?", "The impact of climate change on local species"). One student acts as the "speaker" presenting an idea using target grammar and vocabulary. The other student acts as the "listener" and intentionally uses specific non-verbal cues (e.g., looking skeptical, nodding in agreement, looking confused). The speaker must interpret these cues and adjust their explanation or facilitate discussion by asking questions like "How has deforestation affected our area?" or using community-focused language: "Together, we can make a difference in conservation." Partners provide peer feedback on effective mediation.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[Date]<br>[Time]</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Changing Forests".</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Mediation of "Forest Conservation Report" Sections.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in groups of three. Each group focuses on a specific section of their ongoing "Forest Conservation Report."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Within each group, roles are assigned:
                                <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px;">
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Presenter:</b> Explains a part of their report (e.g., "The forest has lost many native species over the past decade due to illegal logging.") using the present perfect, conditionals, and comparatives.</li>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listener:</b> Reacts to the information with intentional non-verbal cues (e.g., looking confused, frowning, nodding, showing surprise).</li>
                                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Mediator/Facilitator:</b> Observes the listener's non-verbal cues. If confusion is detected, the mediator intervenes to help clarify the presenter's message. This involves rephrasing, simplifying language, asking the presenter to elaborate, or using a quick drawing/analogy. The mediator also facilitates discussion by prompting both presenter and listener to contribute ideas about conservation (e.g., "I noticed you seemed interested in the concept of forest health; would you like to share your thoughts on recycling?"). The mediator should use polite, community-focused language ("Together, we can improve air quality").</li>
                                </ul>
                            </li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups rotate roles, ensuring each student practices mediating, presenting, and actively listening.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Emphasis is placed on using the vocabulary related to deforestation, biodiversity, pollution, conservation, and ecological terms, as well as correct pronunciation of long vowel /aɪ/ and word stress in multisyllabic words like "dramatic" and "ecological."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[Date]<br>[Time]</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and Peer Assessment.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group performs a 2-3 minute mediated discussion on a key finding or recommendation from their "Forest Conservation Report" for the class. The teacher uses an observation checklist, and classmates use a simplified peer assessment rubric.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Mediation:</b> Effectively interprets non-verbal cues and adjusts responses.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Facilitation:</b> Prompts classmates to contribute and clarifies concepts.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Language Use:</b> Accurate use of present perfect, conditionals, and comparatives.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> Appropriate use of target environmental vocabulary (e.g., biodiversity, deforestation, conservation).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation, particularly of /aɪ/ and -ate sounds, and correct word stress.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic/Sociolinguistic:</b> Uses polite and community-focused language to express environmental concerns.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity:</b> Explains complex ideas simply and effectively, possibly using visuals/drawings.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts:
                        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One non-verbal cue I learned to interpret better today was _______, and I responded by _______."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I found it easy/challenging to facilitate discussion about _______ because _______."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[Date]<br>[Time]</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b><br>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did practicing mediation skills help your group communicate ideas from your 'Forest Conservation Report' more clearly?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one specific strategy you will use in future discussions to ensure everyone understands complex environmental concepts?"</li>
                        </ul>
                    </p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in terms of students interpreting non-verbal cues and facilitating discussions? Students demonstrated strong use of conditionals when proposing solutions. The integration of the "Forest Conservation Report" made the mediation task authentic and meaningful. What adjustments are needed? Some students still relied heavily on direct verbal explanations without considering visual aids or rephrasing with simpler vocabulary, especially for terms like 'ecological' or 'approximate.' I will provide more scaffolding for using visuals and simplified language in future mediation tasks. More explicit practice on using community-focused language ('Together, we can...') would also be beneficial.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "Our Changing Forests" unit and the "Forest Conservation Report" project. The next lesson will transition to a new theme, applying similar communicative competencies to a different scenario, focusing on how students can explore other ecological histories.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">[Date]<br>[Time]</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>